hundreds of hong kong police raided the  stan news office in the early hours of  wednesday morning using a warrant under  the much criticized national security  law to collect boxes of what were  described as journalistic materials  six people linked to the independent  media outlet were arrested under a  separate law on sedition from the  british colonial era  by wednesday afternoon stand news  announced it was closing after seven  years we are not they're talking to the  reporter we are not talking the  the media we just targeted national  security  law the offenses  among those arrested was well-known  singer and activist denise ho and former  politician margaret ung both former stan  news board members  police turned up at the home of stan  news deputy assignment editor ronson  chan officers telling him to stop  recording as they arrived chan who is  chairman of the hong kong journalists  association stood by the now disbanded  news organization  has been doing professional reporting  there's no doubt about it the whole  world sees it it doesn't matter what  charges have been made nothing can  change that fact the hong kong  journalists association said it was  deeply concerned by the arrests and  urged the government to respect the  media freedoms that are meant to be  safeguarded under the city's  mini-constitution the basic law  hong kong chief secretary john lee  shrugged off the criticism saying  journalism cannot be used as a tool  against national security they are  the evil elements  that  damage press freedom china's government  in beijing says the security law in hong  kong is necessary for stability  following political unrest and mass  pro-democracy protests  dozens have been arrested prosecuted and  jailed since china imposed the  legislation last year six months ago the  last remaining pro-democracy paper apple  daily was forced to shut down after its  assets were frozen and its founder jimmy  lai arrested  the closure of the apple daily left  stand news is one of the last  pro-democracy outlets in hong kong now  there are questions over whether smaller  online outlets can survive the crackdown  on media freedoms britt clenet al  jazeera hong kong
 

